artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $M$ be a compact Riemannian manifold with cotangent bundle $\pi:T^*M\to M$. Denote by $\omega_0$ the standard symplectic form on $T^*M$ and let $\sigma$ be a closed $2$-form on $M$. Equip $T^*M$ with the twisted symplectic form $\omega=\omega_0+\pi^*\sigma$, and let $H:T^*M\to\mathbb{R}$ be the kinetic-energy Hamiltonian $H(q,p)=\tfrac12|p|^2$ associated with the Riemannian metric. Recall that the Hofer–Zehnder capacity $c_{HZ}(U,\omega)$ of an open set $U\subset T^*M$ is the supremum of $\max F-\min F$ over all smooth functions $F:U\to\mathbb{R}$ that are constant near $\partial U$, vanish near their minimum, attain a maximum on a compact set, and whose Hamiltonian flow on $(U,\omega)$ has no non-constant periodic orbits of period $\le 1$.

Prove or disprove the following statement: For every compact Riemannian manifold $M$ and every closed $2$-form $\sigma$ on $M$,
\begin{enumerate}
\item there exists a neighborhood $U$ of the zero section $M\subset T^*M$ with $c_{HZ}(U,\omega)<\infty$;
\item almost every energy level $\{H=c\}$ (for $c$ in a set of full Lebesgue measure in some interval $(0,\varepsilon)$) carries a periodic orbit of the Hamiltonian flow of $H$ on $(T^*M,\omega)$;
\item if in addition $\sigma$ is symplectic, then every sufficiently low energy level $\{H=c\}$ carries a contractible periodic orbit.
\end{enumerate}

